!

! Source for Site:GroundTemperature:FCfactorMethod: not in the documents. Kept: prototype value, the 12 monthly ground temperatures were generated for the Buffalo weather file by the prototype tools. Not required by the EnergyPlus input syntax, but needed here: the floors use the F-factor method (Construction:FfactorGroundFloor, GroundFCfactorMethod), which takes its ground temperatures from this object; without it EnergyPlus uses a constant default (about 18 C), which understates the ground heat loss in a cold climate. If the weather file changes, these 12 values must be regenerated for the new site.
  Site:GroundTemperature:FCfactorMethod,9.7,6.0,-2.2,-3.4,-4.2,2.7,7.5,13.7,18.6,22.0,20.7,16.5;

!-   ===========  ALL OBJECTS IN CLASS: MATERIAL:REGULAR-R ===========

! Source for Material:NoMass (all 6 objects of this class): layer data (thickness, conductivity, density, thermal resistance, glazing properties) are not in the xlsx; the xlsx Building Summary names only the layer types of the exterior wall and roof, and the PDF gives assembly U-values (see the Construction comments). Kept: prototype values unless a Source comment says so.
  Material:NoMass,
    Opaque Door panel_con,   !- Name
    MediumRough,             !- Roughness
    0.251585991802674,       !- Thermal Resistance {m2-K/W}  Kept: prototype value, not in the xlsx or the PDF (neither gives doors). It is the reciprocal of a door U-factor: R = 1 / (U x 5.678263) = 1 / (0.70 Btu/h-ft2-F x 5.678263 W/m2K per Btu/h-ft2-F) = 0.251586 m2K/W (U = 3.975 W/m2K). 0.70 agrees with the ASHRAE Standard 90.1 U-factor of swinging doors (interpretation, not checked against the standard).
    0.9,                     !- Thermal Absorptance   Default: the value equals the EnergyPlus default of this optional field.
    0.7,                     !- Solar Absorptance   Default: the value equals the EnergyPlus default of this optional field.
    0.7;                     !- Visible Absorptance   Default: the value equals the EnergyPlus default of this optional field.

